The Reed Wardens are the river town's watch on the salt-marsh paths. Twelve wardens work in pairs, patrolling the raised causeways, footbridges, and marked routes between the town's landing and the outer salt pans. They check path markers after high water, close washed-out crossings, escort stranded travelers to firm ground, and investigate theft or violence along these routes.

Wardens carry reed-bound staffs and hooded lanterns. They can stop travelers at a closed crossing and bring suspected offenders before the town's magistrate, but they do not settle cargo ownership or collect trade debts. Their authority ends beyond the maintained marsh paths; they have neither enough boats nor enough people to watch every tidal creek. A patrol can point out a safe marked route, not guarantee passage across flooded ground.

Travelers can ask after the nearest patrol at the landing. For a missing traveler, wardens ask for the last known path, departure time, and identifying details before organizing a search. They need local reports of damaged markers and sudden flooding to keep their routes usable.
{{// so:end}}

Current state: The Reed Wardens maintain paired patrols on the marked marsh paths. No special closure or search is currently established.

