The Ferryman's Tally is a split willow stick used to record and settle river debts for ferry passage and barge haulage. Notches cut across both halves record the agreed amount; the ferryman keeps one half and the debtor keeps the other. When payment is made, the halves are fitted together in front of both parties and the paid notches are crossed out on both halves. A matching pair with all its notches crossed out is proof that the recorded debt has been settled.
The tally is not money and cannot settle an unrelated debt. A lone half can support a claim but cannot prove payment; mismatched grain or notches invalidate the pairing. It records amounts, not the ownership or contents of cargo, and grants no right to seize a crate. New charges require both parties' agreement and fresh matching notches.
Ferrymen want their halves preserved until payment; debtors want the matched, cancelled tally as protection against being charged twice. River traders seek it when settling transport accounts, while debt collectors want uncancelled matching halves as evidence of a claim.
{{// so:end}}
Current state: The Ferryman's Tally remains an accepted way to settle river transport accounts. No particular holder, outstanding amount, or connection to the unsigned crate has been established.

{{// so:protect}}
The Tidewarden's Lantern is a hooded oil lamp carried by marsh guides. Its brass cage protects a thick glass chimney; a shutter narrows the light to a low beam for examining footing without dazzling the guide. A folding hook lets it hang from a staff or a boat's rail.
Guides use it to inspect trail stakes, reed markers and the edges of channels after dark. The lamp burns ordinary lamp oil and needs a trimmed wick, a dry tinder supply and regular cleaning to remove salt deposits. Wind can extinguish it when its hood is open, and immersion puts it out. Its light cannot reveal safe ground beneath water, predict the tide or dispel fog; carrying one does not make an unfamiliar marsh route safe.
Working marsh guides value these lamps as dependable tools. Boat crews and travelers sometimes seek one before a night crossing, but ownership is not proof of a guide's training or authority. Guides commonly keep their own identifying scratches on the base to distinguish borrowed lamps.
{{// so:end}}

Current state: Tidewarden's Lanterns remain in ordinary use among the marsh guides. No particular lamp's owner, location or condition has been established.

{{// so:protect}}
Brine Market is the river town's open-air salt market, where marsh workers sell dried marsh salt by the sack or measured scoop to local buyers and river traders. Its covered stalls stand on raised timber decking above the muddy riverbank.
From the barge landing, a riverside plank walk leads upstream to the market's southern entrance, about five minutes away on foot. The northern gate opens onto the marsh causeway; the eastern lane leads into the town's warehouse streets. On the western river edge, steps descend to a small loading dock, providing another way in or out by shallow-draft boat. At low water the bottom steps meet exposed mud, so boats must wait for enough water to berth.
Salt is weighed at the stalls and kept under cover; rain and river spray can spoil an unprotected load. The loading dock serves market deliveries, not the larger barges that use the landing.
{{// so:end}}
Current state: The Brine Market is operating as the town's regular point of sale for marsh salt. No stallholder has been established as the owner or intended recipient of the unsigned crate at the landing.

{{// so:protect}}
The Tidewarden's Lantern is a hooded oil lamp carried by the salt-marsh guides. Its brass frame shelters a small flame behind thick glass; a sliding shutter lets a guide show a narrow signal or cover the light without extinguishing it. A wire bail allows it to hang from a pole above the reeds.
Guides use the lamp to mark their position and signal companions across nearby channels. It burns ordinary lamp oil and needs a trimmed wick, fuel, and dry tinder to relight. Wind-driven rain can drown an opened lamp, and salt deposits cloud its glass unless cleaned. Its light cannot reveal submerged paths, measure water depth, predict tides, or guarantee safe footing; fog and tall reeds sharply limit its visibility.
Marsh guides want reliable lamps for night crossings, and boat crews seek their signals when approaching a landing after dark. Carrying one is not proof that its bearer knows a safe route: the lamp can be borrowed, bought, or stolen.
{{// so:end}}
Current state: Tidewarden's Lanterns are working equipment carried by marsh guides around the river trading town. No particular lamp's owner, location, or condition has yet been established.

