The Barge Strike lasted one trading season. The bargemen collectively refused to carry cargo after quay merchants imposed lower freight payments while leaving crews responsible for losses in transit. Barges remained moored and the town's regular river trade stopped; the river itself was neither dammed nor made impassable. Small private boats could still travel, but could not replace the barges' carrying capacity.

Salt accumulated in riverside stores, imported grain grew scarce, and traders paid more to move goods overland along the marsh edge. Crews supported one another from pooled provisions, while some households left to find work elsewhere. Trade resumed after the merchants and crew delegates agreed on freight rates and limits to crews' liability for spoiled cargo. Resumption did not erase the season's debts or restore every lost business.

What people say: Bargemen remember the stoppage as proof that the town depends on their labour; some merchant families call it a season of deliberate starvation. Stories credit a single brave captain or blame outside agitators. These are competing accounts, not established explanations: the stoppage involved crews acting together, and shortages had several causes. No concealed instigator or secret bargain is established in this shared history.
{{// so:end}}

Current state: River cargo service has resumed. The strike remains a point of dispute whenever freight rates, cargo liability or the price of staple goods are discussed.

{{// so:protect}}
The Saltmen’s Guild is a small local association of salt cutters and carriers working the marsh beside the river trading town. Its crews cut salt crust from shallow drying beds, drain and sort it, then carry it in lined baskets to covered stores at the landing. Barges take the weighed salt upriver. Wet salt must be dried again before it can be sold by weight.
The guild numbers several dozen workers, organized into crews rather than a standing guard. A crew foreman allocates beds and carrying shifts; an elected tally keeper records loads, wages, and deliveries. Their influence reaches the marsh paths, salt stores, and salt-loading berths, not the whole town or distant river ports. They can withhold their labor and refuse to certify a salt load, but cannot lawfully seize unrelated cargo or command the town watch.
Members seek reliable pay, safe marsh paths, and honest weights. Buyers and barge crews deal with the tally keeper over loads and receipts. A guild receipt identifies the delivering crew, weight, and intended buyer; a salt-stained crate alone proves neither guild ownership nor its destination.
{{// so:end}}
Current state: The guild is cutting and carrying marsh salt during workable tides and daylight. Its tally keeper handles routine questions about salt deliveries at the landing; disputed loads remain unclaimed until their records can be checked.

{{// so:protect}}
Gullwater Locks are the old river locks upstream of the river trading town, above the salt marsh. A towpath connects the town's upper landing to the locks; on foot the journey takes about half an hour. Barges approach along the main river channel from downstream or the higher freshwater reach upstream. A narrow footbridge across the lower gates links the two banks.

Two stone-walled chambers lift or lower boats between the river levels. Boats must pass through one chamber at a time, with its gates closed before water is admitted or drained through the sluices. The gates and sluices are worked by hand from the lockside. The footbridge is for pedestrians, not carts; the towpath is the practical overland route back to town. Swift water near an open sluice makes swimming or handling a small boat beside it dangerous.
{{// so:end}}

Current state: The locks remain passable, but worn gate gear makes passage slow. Barges wait at the mooring posts below the lower chamber until the way is clear; the towpath and footbridge are open.

{{// so:protect}}
Marsh Sight is a learned knack some marsh locals have for reading nearby conditions from fog: its drift against reeds, breaks over channels, changing density and the way sound carries through it. Experienced readers can infer a likely watercourse, an approaching bank of thicker fog, or whether a familiar crossing is becoming unsafe. They must observe the actual fog and compare several signs; a single swirl proves little.
The knack is local and fallible, not prophecy or vision through mist. It cannot reveal names, intentions, hidden cargo or distant events, and it does not identify who owns an unclaimed crate. Unfamiliar ground, shifting winds, heavy rain and a turning tide can defeat even a practiced reader. A reading describes conditions at that moment, not a guaranteed safe route later. Readers still sound the water and check landmarks before committing to a crossing.
Learning requires repeated journeys with an experienced reader. Taking a careful reading costs time and attention; watching fog instead of footing or boat traffic creates immediate risks. Locals who know the knack may disagree and should explain the signs behind their judgments rather than claim certainty.
{{// so:end}}
Current state: Some locals use Marsh Sight as a practical aid to marsh travel. No reading of the present fog has yet been established.

