The Drowning of Old Quay was the flood that destroyed the river town's first harbour. Days of rain swelled the river while a storm tide drove seawater inland through the salt marsh. With the river unable to empty, water overtopped the quay and broke through the bank behind its warehouses. The flood washed away timber landing stages, filled the harbour basin with silt and wrecked the storehouses along the waterfront. People escaped toward higher ground, but not everyone reached it.
The survivors established the working landing farther upriver on firmer ground rather than rebuild the first harbour. Old Quay remained outside the repaired banks, exposed to tidal water. Broken mooring stones and the foundations of warehouses mark the former waterfront at low water; mud and submerged timbers make approaching them dangerous.
Local accounts sometimes call the flood a punishment for the harbour's greed. That is a remembered interpretation, not an established cause: the disaster followed heavy river flooding meeting a storm tide. No single agreed count of the dead survives.
{{// so:end}}
Current state: Old Quay is a tidal ruin, not a working harbour. River traders use the newer landing. The name 'the Drowning' still refers to the loss of the first harbour; exposed ruins remain a reminder of the flood.

